{%- comment -%}
  fye-gem-swatch — one gemstone swatch, drawn not photographed.

  Added 05/09/2026. Ed: "use the existing colour icons for the gems to do the
  diamond or gem picker screen."

  The colours ALREADY existed, but only as inline hex inside
  snippets/mm-gemstones.liquid — the mega-menu's stone grid. Copying them into
  the finder would have created a second set to keep in step, and the first
  time someone corrected a colour the two would disagree. So they live here
  once, and mm-gemstones should be pointed at this snippet next time it is
  touched (not today: that snippet is matched to live and is not what Ed asked
  about).

  USAGE — the finder already emits the shared gradients for the metal swatch,
  and this reuses them, so there is nothing extra to render once per page:

    {%- render 'fye-gem-swatch', gem: 'Sapphire', class: 'rfd__oicon' -%}

  PARAMS
    gem     'Sapphire', 'Blue Topaz', 'Diamond', … Case and spacing are
            forgiving. An unknown name falls back to the pale ground with a
            hairline rather than rendering nothing, so a typo in the theme
            editor shows as a blank stone instead of a missing tile.
    class   the consumer's own class, so sizing stays with the consumer's CSS —
            same contract as fye-metal-swatch.

  CONSTRUCTION is deliberately identical to fye-metal-swatch: same 30x30 box,
  same 25x25 square, same diagonal sheen over the colour, same hairline. That
  is the point — the stone question and the metal question are the same kind of
  question, and they should not look like two different controls.

  THREE COLOURS ARE NEW, and are mine rather than the menu's, because the
  finder offers stones the mega-menu does not: Diamond, Fire Opal and Black
  Diamond. Flagged so they can be corrected by someone who sells them:
    diamond        near-colourless, carried by the sheen alone
    fire-opal      warm orange, between citrine and ruby
    black-diamond  near-black with a cool cast, not flat black
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign key = gem | strip | downcase | replace: ' ', '-'
  comment
    Two of the finder's names are the menu's stone with a qualifier. Blue topaz
    IS the menu's topaz; pink tourmaline IS its tourmaline. Mapping them keeps
    one colour per stone.
  endcomment
  if key == 'blue-topaz'
    assign key = 'topaz'
  elsif key == 'pink-tourmaline'
    assign key = 'tourmaline'
  endif

  case key
    when 'ruby'
      assign hex = '#9B1B2E'
    when 'sapphire'
      assign hex = '#1B3A6B'
    when 'emerald'
      assign hex = '#1B6B45'
    when 'tanzanite'
      assign hex = '#5B4FA8'
    when 'aquamarine'
      assign hex = '#8FC8DC'
    when 'morganite'
      assign hex = '#E8AFA8'
    when 'peridot'
      assign hex = '#9BB93B'
    when 'amethyst'
      assign hex = '#8B4FA8'
    when 'garnet'
      assign hex = '#7B1B25'
    when 'citrine'
      assign hex = '#E0A128'
    when 'topaz'
      assign hex = '#3E92CC'
    when 'opal'
      assign hex = '#EDE8DE'
    when 'tourmaline'
      assign hex = '#D9629B'
    when 'diamond'
      assign hex = '#F4F6F7'
    when 'fire-opal'
      assign hex = '#D9761F'
    when 'black-diamond'
      assign hex = '#2A2C2E'
    else
      assign hex = '#EDE8DE'
  endcase
-%}
<svg class="fic fic--gem fic--gem-{{ key }} {{ class }}" viewBox="0 0 30 30" role="img" aria-label="{{ gem | escape }}">
  <rect x="2.5" y="2.5" width="25" height="25" fill="{{ hex }}"></rect>
  {%- comment -%}
    The same sheen the metal swatches use, so a stone and a metal read as the
    same object drawn twice. The finder renders the gradient defs already.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <rect x="2.5" y="2.5" width="25" height="25" fill="url(#fic-sheen)"></rect>
  <rect x="2.5" y="2.5" width="25" height="25" fill="none" stroke="var(--metal-edge)" stroke-width="1"></rect>
</svg>
